#! /bin/sh
# /etc/init.d/vlhe: load the VLHE virtual devices and start their daemons.
#
# Copyright (c) 2026 Thomas Tranter
# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
#
# Part of VLHE. See LICENSE for the full license text.
#
# NEVER RUN THIS ON A WORKSTATION. It insmods modules built for
# 2.2.16 and starts daemons that open /dev/dsp, /dev/mixer and
# /dev/vdiscctl. `vlhe apply' refuses a machine that is not 2.2.16
# with a Corel /etc/modules, and that check is the only thing between
# this script and a modern kernel - do not add a way past it.
#
# WHAT IT IS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. This is a thin wrapper
# around `vlhe apply'. Every decision - which module first, which
# parameter where, which daemon with which flags - is made by
# vlhe_apply.c from /etc/vlhe.conf, is a pure function of that file,
# and is checked line by line by a host test. Reimplementing any of
# it here would be a second copy to keep in step, and the copy that
# runs at boot is the one nobody watches.
#
# So this script decides three things only:
#
#   1. whether to run at all (LoadAtBoot, and the binaries existing)
#   2. what the user sees while it happens
#   3. what happens when a step fails
#
# INSTALL IT AS /etc/rcS.d/S60vlhe, WITH K40vlhe IN /etc/rc0.d,
# /etc/rc1.d AND /etc/rc6.d FOR THE STOP. Not in rc.boot and not with a dot
# in the name: `run-parts' silently skips any filename containing a
# dot - CLAUDE.md section 4 records `dma.sh' never running and saying
# nothing - and /etc/init.d/rcS calls rc.boot last, describing it in
# its own comment as "for compatibility".
#
#   ln -s ../init.d/vlhe /etc/rcS.d/S60vlhe
#   ln -s ../init.d/vlhe /etc/rc0.d/K40vlhe
#   ln -s ../init.d/vlhe /etc/rc1.d/K40vlhe
#   ln -s ../init.d/vlhe /etc/rc6.d/K40vlhe
#
# (`update-rc.d vlhe start 60 S . stop 40 0 1 6 .' makes the same four.)
#
# ON RED HAT (no rcS.d) IT IS /etc/rc.d/init.d/vlhe, S60 in rc2.d-rc5.d
# and K40 in rc0.d, rc1.d and rc6.d - `make install-boot' places either
# layout. See the lock file under `start' for why K40 needs it there.
#
# WHY S60 - DECIDED 2026-10-02, AND THIS SAID S11 UNTIL THEN. S11 was
# chosen to run before S20modutils because the mixer had to load
# before the card's driver - which stopped being true with the
# /dev/dsp symlink design (2026-09-25: the card never moves, load
# order does not matter). And S11 had become HARMFUL twice over:
#
#   - the card's driver is not loaded until S20modutils reads
#     /etc/modules, so the pump had no card to open; and
#   - S55bootmisc.sh runs `find /var/run ! -type d -exec rm -f', which
#     DELETES /var/run/vlhe/ctl/*.pid and the daemons' control channels.
#     Started before it, VLHE booted apparently fine and the Status
#     page and the GUI lost every daemon at S55.
#
# Corel's rcS.d README: "After the S40 scripts have executed ... All
# device drivers have been initialized." S60 is after both S40 and
# S55, and long before kdm (S99, in the runlevel). design/41 has the
# account.
#
# WHY K40 - Corel's hand-placed services stop at 100 minus their start
# (S89cron/K11cron, S10sysklogd/K90sysklogd), the reverse of start
# order. rcS.d never calls `stop' (rcS iterates S??* only), so without
# the K links the /dev/dsp redirect outlived every shutdown. K40 runs
# after the network services and before K90sysklogd, so the stop can
# still log; every K runs before S20sendsigs and S40umountfs.
#
# AND rc1.d, SINGLE USER - added 2026-10-02 at the user's instruction.
# Corel's rc1.d stops every service with the same K list as rc0.d and
# rc6.d, then S20single runs `killall5 -15', `killall5 -9' and `exec
# init -t1 S'. Without a K link there, switching to single user KILLED
# the daemons and left the modules loaded and /dev/dsp pointing at
# vsound with no pump behind it. K40 stops it properly first.
#
# WHY NOT A RUNLEVEL DIRECTORY FOR THE START. rcS runs once, before any
# runlevel. It runs on a SINGLE-USER BOOT too, and this used to call
# that a feature ("where the machine's devices belong"); the user
# decided otherwise on 2026-10-02, so `start' skips a single-user
# boot - see SINGLE USER IS SKIPPED below. A K link in rc1.d cannot
# cover that case: Corel's /etc/init.d/rc runs K scripts only when
# PREVLEVEL is not N, and at boot it is N.
#
# AND vsoundd MUST PRECEDE X. With the module loaded and the pump not
# running, audio goes into the channel and nowhere else: it appears
# to work and is silent. `vlhe apply' starts the pump, so being in
# rcS covers it - kdm is S99 in a runlevel directory, long after.
#
# C89 has nothing to do with this file, but the shell does: STOCK
# COREL IS bash 2.01.1 and the Acer is 2.03. Nothing here uses a
# construct newer than that, and nothing uses `printf' - sh-utils
# 1.16's segfaults on a width after a numeric conversion (design/07),
# and `echo' is enough for everything this prints.

set -e

PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
export PATH
# THE DEFAULT IFS - design/55 recommendation 3, 2026-10-04. A plain `su'
# or `dpkg -r' carries the caller's IFS in, and everything below splits
# words with it. The CLI this runs drops every VLHE_* itself when root
# (vlhe_self_root_env()); the two below are also unset here, by name.
IFS=' 	
'
# THE BINARY IS `vlhe' - RENAMED 2026-10-05 to the names design/09
# decided ("NAMING - DECIDED 2026-09-16"): `vlhe' the CLI, `vlhe.gtk'
# the GUI. Until then the CLI built as `vlhe-cli' and this looked for
# both names; it now looks for `vlhe' only, in sbin then local sbin.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INSTALLED BUILD ONLY - design/55 13f, the user's two-builds
# decision of 2026-10-04.
#
# THIS SCRIPT USED TO DRIVE A PORTABLE TREE TOO: the tree it sat in
# (a PORTABLE marker beside $0), or one named in the environment -
# `VLHE_DIR=/mnt/xfer /etc/init.d/vlhe start'. Both were the hybrid
# the decision removes - system machinery (root, at boot, from rcS)
# running a folder's programs and modules - and VLHE_DIR was design/55
# R1's route: a plain `su' or `dpkg -r' carries it in from someone's
# environment, and root then ran that folder's `vlhe'. The user had not
# known it existed; a portable copy is run from its folder by hand and
# never at boot. So: the installed CLI, the installed config, and no
# variable that points anywhere else.
#
# VLHE_DIR and VLHE_MODULE_DIR are unset rather than ignored, so the
# CLI this runs cannot read them either.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# `|| true' ON THE unset, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING - 2026-10-05. Corel's
# bash (2.01.1 stock, 2.03 on the 86Box guest) makes `unset' of a
# variable that is NOT set return failure, and under `set -e' that
# ends the script on the spot with no message. Modern bash returns 0,
# so every host test passed; on the target this script exited at
# this line for EVERY verb - nothing loaded at boot, and `stop' at
# shutdown and in the package's prerm did nothing either. Measured under the
# stock 2.01.1: `set -e; unset NOPE' exits 1, and `unset NOPE X ||
# true' still unsets X and carries on.
unset VLHE_DIR VLHE_MODULE_DIR || true

VLHE=
for c in /usr/sbin/vlhe /usr/local/sbin/vlhe; do
    if [ -x "$c" ]; then
        VLHE=$c
        break
    fi
done

# AND SAY SO WHEN ASKED DIRECTLY. A boot must stay quiet, but someone
# typing `/etc/init.d/vlhe start' at a prompt deserves to know why
# nothing happened. `$1' is empty at boot only in the sense that
# run-parts always passes one - so this fires exactly when a person
# ran it.
# `start' ONLY, 2026-10-02 - see the no-config guard below for why.
if [ -z "$VLHE" ] && [ -t 1 ] && [ "$1" = start ]; then
    echo "vlhe: not installed - no vlhe in /usr/sbin" >&2
    echo "      or /usr/local/sbin. A portable copy is not started" >&2
    echo "      from here - run it from its folder." >&2
fi

# THE INSTALLED CONFIG - a portable tree's own vlhe.conf is not this
# script's business any more (above).
CONF=/etc/vlhe.conf

NAME="VLHE virtual devices"

# WHICH COPY OF THIS SCRIPT IS THIS? Substituted by the staging
# scripts (mkheader.sh's stamp_file) and left saying "unstaged" in the
# repo, which is the truth for a file that has not been staged.
#
# THE USER ASKED FOR IT, 2026-09-22, after the init script failed the
# same way twice and the question was whether the guest was running
# the fix at all: "should we update the init script so it prints the
# date time to log ... like a build or commit number something we can
# use to check that it is current."
#
# A DATE ALONE WOULD NOT HAVE ANSWERED IT. Two restages minutes apart
# both look current; the commit says WHICH tree. `-dirty' means it was
# built from uncommitted work and matches no commit at all.
#
# IT IS PRINTED, NOT CHECKED. Nothing here compares it against
# anything - the script cannot know what the newest build is, and a
# version check that guesses would be worse than a line a person can
# read. See the `status' verb, which is where to look for it after the
# boot has scrolled past.
VLHE_STAMP="unstaged"

# THE BINARY MAY NOT BE INSTALLED, and an init script for a package
# that has been removed but not purged must exit quietly rather than
# printing an error on every boot. This is the `test -f ... || exit 0'
# idiom every Debian script opens with, and sysklogd's own first two
# lines are exactly it.
test -n "$VLHE" || exit 0

# AND WITHOUT A CONFIG THERE IS NOTHING TO APPLY. Not an error: a
# machine that has installed the package and not yet configured it is
# in a perfectly good state, and `vlhe apply' would emit a plan built
# entirely from defaults - loading modules the user never asked for.
#
# BUT SAY SO WHEN A PERSON TYPED THE COMMAND. The same `[ -t 1 ]'
# test the missing-binary message above uses, and for the same
# reason: a boot must stay quiet, and someone at a prompt deserves to
# know why nothing happened. Exiting mute is what made this cost an
# evening.
#
# AND ONLY FOR `start' - 2026-10-02. Both hints are about LOADING
# ("nothing to load", "install the package"), and both fired on `stop'
# too: `dpkg --purge vlhe' printed this one from the prerm's
# `/etc/init.d/vlhe stop', because dpkg runs it on a terminal - "or
# install the package" while removing it (tests/logs/
# 2026-10-02-86box-deb-first-install/RUN.txt). The missing-binary one
# above would have fired at every shutdown after a `dpkg -r', when the
# conffile and its K links stay and the programs are gone. A person
# typing `stop' with nothing configured has nothing to be told.
#
# AND `stop' IS NOT GUARDED AT ALL - 2026-10-02, the user: "Fix the
# dpkg to remove the modules as well". Unloading needs no settings:
# plan_unload() stops the daemons by their pid files and removes only
# the modules /proc/modules says are loaded (loaded_or_assumed()), and
# /dev/dsp goes back from the saved dsp.was. But this guard exited
# first, so VLHE loaded from the control centre and never SAVED left
# no /etc/vlhe.conf - and the package's prerm `stop' then did nothing,
# so `dpkg -r' removed the programs with the modules still loaded and
# /dev/dsp still pointing at vsound. Nothing loaded is fine too: the
# plan is then empty.
if [ ! -f "$CONF" ] && [ "$1" != stop ]; then
    if [ -t 1 ] && [ "$1" = start ]; then
        echo "vlhe: no configuration found, so there is nothing to" >&2
        echo "      load. Looked for:" >&2
        echo "        /etc/vlhe.conf" >&2
        echo "" >&2
        echo "      Run the control centre and save your settings," >&2
        echo "      or install the package." >&2
    fi
    exit 0
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Should we run at all?
#
# EACH COMPONENT HAS ITS OWN LoadAtBoot (the user, 2026-09-11) and
# `vlhe apply' honours them - it builds the plan from the config, so a
# component set off simply does not appear in it. THIS SCRIPT DOES NOT
# READ THEM, deliberately: parsing an ini file in sh to make a
# decision the C code already makes correctly is exactly the second
# copy this file exists to avoid.
#
# What it does check is whether ANY of them is on, so that a machine
# with everything disabled prints nothing rather than announcing a
# plan with no steps in it.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
anything_at_boot() {
    # `LoadAtBoot = 1' under any section. A user who has set them all
    # to 0 gets silence; anything else, including a malformed file,
    # errs towards running - vlhe apply is the thing that understands
    # the format and it will say if it cannot read it.
    grep -q '^[ 	]*LoadAtBoot[ 	]*=[ 	]*[1-9]' "$CONF" || return 1
    return 0
}

case "$1" in
  start)
    anything_at_boot || exit 0

    # SINGLE USER IS SKIPPED AT BOOT - 2026-10-02, the user's decision.
    # Single user is for repairing the machine, and S20single kills
    # every daemon anyway, so loading VLHE there only leaves its
    # modules and the /dev/dsp redirect behind with nothing running.
    #
    # THE KERNEL COMMAND LINE IS WHAT ASKS FOR IT, read the way init
    # reads its own arguments (sysvinit 2.76 init.c:2397-2406):
    # `single', `s' or `S' mean S, and `1' is runlevel 1, which is
    # what Corel's own Single_User entry passes (lilo.conf
    # append="1"). Whole words only - `root=/dev/hda1' is not a `1'.
    # /proc is mounted by S10checkroot.sh, long before S60.
    #
    # ONLY AT BOOT, told apart by $0: rcS runs `/etc/rcS.d/S60vlhe
    # start' (its loop is `$i start'), so $0 names the rcS.d link then
    # and never when a person types /etc/init.d/vlhe start - which, in
    # a single-user shell, is someone asking for VLHE on purpose.
    # RUNLEVEL cannot tell them apart: it is `S' during rcS and in the
    # single-user shell alike (init.c:82).
    case "$0" in
        */rcS.d/*)
            if [ -r /proc/cmdline ]; then
                for _w in `cat /proc/cmdline`; do
                    case "$_w" in
                        single|s|S|1)
                            echo "VLHE: single-user boot - not starting."
                            exit 0 ;;
                    esac
                done
            fi
            ;;
    esac

    echo -n "Starting $NAME:"

    # INTO THE JOURNAL, NOT ONLY THE CONSOLE. The boot message scrolls
    # past and 2.2's syslog may never flush it (CLAUDE.md section 2 -
    # a hard-reset machine's log is cut at a flush boundary, not at
    # the failure), so the console copy is the one that cannot be
    # relied on. The journal is a plain appended file that `vlhe
    # apply' is about to write to anyway.
    #
    # BEST EFFORT, AND FAILURE IS IGNORED. At S60 every local
    # filesystem is mounted (S35mountall), so /var/log is normally
    # writable - this was written for S11, when it might not have
    # been - but a stamp that cannot be written is still not a reason
    # to skip the apply.
    for d in /var/log/vlhe; do
        if [ -d "$d" ] && [ -w "$d" ]; then
            echo "boot `date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'` init script $VLHE_STAMP" \
                >> "$d/changes" 2>/dev/null || true
            break
        fi
    done

    # RED HAT'S LOCK FILE - 2026-10-06. Red Hat's /etc/rc.d/rc runs a
    # K script at shutdown ONLY if /var/lock/subsys/<name> exists, and
    # rc.sysinit empties that directory at every boot (initscripts
    # 4.16, Red Hat 6.0) - so without this the K40 links install-boot
    # places there never ran, and /dev/dsp stayed redirected. Made
    # before the apply, so even a partial load is stopped. Where the
    # directory does not exist (Corel) nothing happens.
    if [ -d /var/lock/subsys ]; then
        touch /var/lock/subsys/vlhe 2>/dev/null || true
    fi

    # `set -e' IS OFF FOR THE APPLY, and this is decision 3 from
    # design/09. A failure inside the plan is not a reason to abort
    # the boot: the pump without the disc daemons is useful, and a
    # machine that stops booting because a disc image moved is worse
    # than one that comes up without that disc. vlhe apply already
    # distinguishes optional steps from required ones and stops
    # itself at a required failure.
    set +e
    # --boot: FIRST FINISH A LOAD THAT WAS NEVER UNLOADED (a power loss
    # or a crash while loaded), under [Boot] FinishLeftover - design/54
    # 7h decision 2. What it did goes to the one-time notice the Status
    # page and `vlhe status' show, since this output goes nowhere.
    "$VLHE" apply --boot >/dev/null 2>&1
    rc=$?
    set -e

    if [ $rc -eq 0 ]; then
        echo " done."
    else
        # NAMED, NOT SILENT, AND IT DOES NOT FAIL THE BOOT. The
        # journal is the thing that says WHAT happened - this line
        # only has to make the user look at it.
        echo " FAILED - see /var/log/vlhe/changes"
    fi
    ;;

  stop)
    # UNCONDITIONAL, unlike start. LoadAtBoot says whether to bring
    # something UP; on the way down we tear down whatever is actually
    # there, because a user who started a daemon by hand still wants
    # it stopped before the filesystems go.
    echo -n "Stopping $NAME:"

    # THE OUTPUT IS KEPT, NOT DISCARDED - changed 2026-09-22. It went
    # to /dev/null and the failure line then said "some parts remain"
    # with no way to learn WHICH, pointing at a journal that had
    # nothing about the stop in it. The user: "the output is not
    # helpful it's complaining and I dont know what".
    #
    # A TEMPORARY RATHER THAN A PIPE, because a pipe would make $? the
    # exit status of `tail' and the whole point here is the exit
    # status of the apply.
    #
    # IN A ROOT-ONLY DIRECTORY, NOT /tmp - design/55 recommendation 5,
    # removing R5 (2026-10-04). It was /tmp/vlhe-stop.$$, written with
    # `>' as root: any user could leave a symlink at that name for a
    # likely pid, and every shutdown, reboot or `dpkg -r' would then
    # truncate and overwrite the file it named. The target has no
    # mktemp. Only root can create in /var/run, so a directory there
    # made 0700 by root is safe to write in - and if it cannot be
    # confirmed as a plain directory, the output is discarded as it
    # was before 2026-09-22 rather than written somewhere unsafe.
    outdir=/var/run/vlhe-init
    mkdir -m 0700 "$outdir" 2>/dev/null || true
    if [ -d "$outdir" ] && [ ! -h "$outdir" ]; then
        chmod 0700 "$outdir" 2>/dev/null || true
        out="$outdir/stop.out"
        rm -f "$out"
    else
        out=/dev/null
    fi

    set +e
    "$VLHE" apply -u > "$out" 2>&1
    rc=$?
    set -e

    if [ $rc -eq 0 ]; then
        echo " done."
    else
        # A MODULE THAT WILL NOT COME OUT IS NORMAL AT SHUTDOWN -
        # something may still hold a device. Nothing here should make
        # a shutdown hang or fail on that, so this reports and
        # continues.
        echo " some parts remain:"
        # THE FAILING STEPS THEMSELVES. `vlhe apply' prints one line
        # per step and marks a failure; showing those beats naming a
        # log file, and this is a console a user is looking at.
        if [ "$out" != /dev/null ]; then
            sed -n 's/^/    /p' "$out" 2>/dev/null | tail -12
        fi
        echo "    (full detail: /var/log/vlhe/changes)"
    fi
    [ "$out" = /dev/null ] || rm -f "$out"
    # Red Hat's lock (see start): gone, so rc does not stop it twice.
    rm -f /var/lock/subsys/vlhe 2>/dev/null || true
    ;;

  restart|force-reload)
    # design/09 names start|stop|restart|reload|force-reload as the
    # set, modelled on kerneld. `restart' is the only one with real
    # work behind it.
    $0 stop
    # A PAUSE BETWEEN THEM, and it is not superstition: rmmod of
    # vsound can only succeed once the daemons have released their
    # channels, and `vlhe apply -u' returns when it has asked them to
    # stop rather than when the kernel has finished. One second is
    # the same order as the 5 s timeout stop_daemon() already applies
    # per daemon.
    sleep 1
    $0 start
    ;;

  reload)
    # NOT IMPLEMENTED, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. design/09 wants
    # /etc/init.d/skeleton's `reload)' case built - "it would let the
    # GUI apply a config change without stopping audio" - and nothing
    # implements it: vmidid and vdiscd take their settings as
    # command-line arguments parsed once at exec, with no config
    # reread and no signal handler. A `reload' that quietly did a
    # restart would stop the user's audio without warning; one that
    # quietly did nothing would be worse.
    echo "$NAME: reload is not supported - use restart" >&2
    exit 3
    ;;

  status)
    # NOT A STANDARD DEBIAN VERB in this era, and cheap to offer:
    # `vlhe status' already exists and is the thing a user reaches
    # for after a failed boot.
    #
    # THE SCRIPT'S OWN STAMP GOES FIRST, because `vlhe status' reports
    # on the BINARY and the machine, and this is the one line that
    # says anything about the file being read right now. A target
    # running a stale init script is exactly the case this answers,
    # and it is the first thing to rule out when a fix appears not to
    # have taken.
    echo "init script: $VLHE_STAMP"
    echo "binary:      $VLHE"
    echo ""
    exec "$VLHE" status
    ;;

  *)
    echo "Usage: /etc/init.d/vlhe {start|stop|restart|force-reload|status}" >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

exit 0
